A||||8||Loki||Setting TPS?!?!?!||||1||
Z||000000||Loki||08-27-2001||10:18 PM||||I just put on new TPS and IAC on my black 86SE.. I got the IAC set I think. But someone said you have to set the TPS with an ohm meter????  Do I, or just slap it on???<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/loki-mg/loki_sig.jpg">  <BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>http://members.home.net/loki-mg||||reg||1
Z||000001||Joe Torma||08-27-2001||10:44 PM||||You need to tweak the arm so the TPS produced about .54V<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Loki||08-27-2001||11:04 PM||||Tweak? As in how?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe Torma||08-27-2001||11:20 PM||||I guess i have to remember to be more specific.  Get 1 needle nose and 1 regular pliers.  the arm is L shaped grab the short part and hold tight with the regular pliers--you don't want to pull the arm off or upset it.  Grab the other leg and tweak it up or down to change the value.  You shouldn't have to move it much.  If you do, then your idlw screw is way off.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Loki||08-27-2001||11:54 PM||||OK then, how and where do I check for the .54v?||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||08-28-2001||01:55 AM||||The TPS has 3 terminals.  With the ignition switched on, one termninal will give you +5 volts, another will give you zero volts (ground).  <P>The terminal you're looking for will give you a voltage that varies with throttle position.  I don't remember which one it is, but logic would tell me the center terminal is probably a good guess.<P>Anyway, if you unplug the connector from the TPS, turn the ignition on and check the voltage at each of the terminals in the connector, one should read +5 volts.  Another terminal should give you zero volts; it's probably got a black wire going to it.  That's the ground.  The remaining terminal, then, is the one you want to take your output reading from; if you take a reading with the connector unplugged, it'll probably read zero, or possibly a very small voltage.       <P>When you determine which terminal to take the reading from, plug the connector into the TPS, turn the ignition on, and bend the little tang on the TPS to get the desired reading (with the throttle closed).||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||08-28-2001||09:28 AM||||Actually, just make sure that the throttle lever for the TPS is in contact with the TPS actuator at the idle stop.  The computer takes a base reading when the ignition is turned on as the idle point.  Unless the new TPS is junk, it should work fine.  I did have a Standard part that had a low reading and it couldn't be adjusted out.  They had had a bad lot at the manufacturer. GL||||reg||1
Z||000007||Joe Torma||08-28-2001||10:48 AM||||There is a range specified in the service manual.  Set too high or low and it sets a code.  I've always used .54v<P>Joe<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||pollock||08-28-2001||06:55 PM||||"What's this about you having problems with your TPS reports?"<P>"Yeah, didn't you get that memo?"<P>--Office Space||||reg||10
